schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "What Is Cryptocurrency?"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    A plain-language introduction to cryptocurrency for community organizers
    and growers, using a "shared notebook" analogy for blockchain, drawing
    the coin/token distinction, and grounding the "why should I care"
    question in four concrete cases (Sarafu Network local currency, Regen
    Network ecological credits, Glo Dollar's donation-funding stablecoin,
    and cheap cross-border remittances), before addressing head-on the three
    standard fears: environmental impact, scams, and hype.
  audience: community organisers and growers new to crypto (gardens, tool libraries, seed swaps)
  known_tensions:
    - decentralisation-as-no-single-boss vs the practical need for someone to still write good software and maintain it
    - crypto's real environmental cost is chain-specific (proof-of-work vs proof-of-stake), not a single verdict
    - the honest "you almost certainly don't need crypto" framing vs the four cases presented as genuinely useful
  related_concepts:
    - blockchain-shared-ledger
    - coins-vs-tokens
  related_resources:
    - Sarafu Network / Grassroots Economics
    - Regen Network
    - Glo Dollar
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-is-cryptocurrency.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  source_lineage: what-is-cryptocurrency.md
  notes: |
    # assessed-but-excluded
    - GiveDirectly is mentioned only as "a major recipient" of Glo Dollar's
      donated interest, a brief supporting detail folded into the Glo
      Dollar resource note rather than given its own object.
    - Bankless Academy and Kaspersky are cited only as general educational
      references (a lesson platform and a definitional source), not
      elaborated on in the body; excluded as their own resource objects to
      avoid mega-listing thin mentions.
    - no people/community profiles, exact locations, TEK, or funding/legal
      recommendations named; standard scam-safety guidance is not a
      high-risk trigger under the framework's list.
